description:
In AttachmentServlet.doGet, after the permission check and 304 handling, the servlet writes a Content-Disposition header built by string-concatenating att.getFileName() directly into the header value:

    res.addHeader( "Content-Disposition", "inline; filename=\"" + att.getFileName() + "\";" );

att.getFileName() is derived from the stored attachment name rather than from a hard-coded value (compare with the 304 check at line 253, which also uses att.getName()). If an attachment name can contain CR/LF or quote characters, the value reaches the header sink unencoded. Servlet containers vary in whether they reject CR/LF inside setHeader/addHeader, so this is a candidate, not a confirmed vulnerability. The same doGet method does show that the author was aware of the sensitivity of user-supplied values in this flow: the 'nextpage' parameter is routed through a validateNextPage(...) helper before sendRedirect (line 304), while the filename is not routed through any visible encoding/sanitization step. The upload-side path that would normally sanitize the attachment name (AttachmentManager / BasicAttachmentProvider) was not within the readable spans, so whether a name containing CR/LF can actually be persisted and later read back here is unverified.

AttachmentServlet.doGet reads the request parameter "nextpage" (line 204) and, after successfully streaming the attachment, issues a redirect to it:

    if( nextPage != null ) {
        res.sendRedirect( validateNextPage( nextPage, m_engine.getURL( WikiContext.ERROR, "", null, false ) ) );
    }

So the user-controlled parameter is passed as the redirect target. The presence of a validateNextPage helper suggests the author intended to constrain the destination (the second argument, the engine's ERROR URL, looks like the intended fallback), but the body of validateNextPage was not within the readable spans, so it is not established whether the check rejects absolute/external URLs (e.g. "//evil.example", "https://evil.example") or protocol tricks. This is a candidate open-redirect / redirect-to-arbitrary-URL weakness contingent on the helper's actual logic; the code as read simply forwards the parameter value into sendRedirect.

In WikiJSPFilter.doFilter the raw request URI is concatenated into logging and watchdog state without any visible encoding:

    NDC.push( m_engine.getApplicationName()+":"+((HttpServletRequest)request).getRequestURI() );
    w.enterState("Filtering for URL "+((HttpServletRequest)request).getRequestURI(), 90 );

The value is the request URI (attacker-controllable path), and it flows into NDC (log4j nested diagnostic context) and the watchdog state string. This is only a candidate for log-injection/response-encoding issues: whether it is exploitable depends on the logging configuration and on whether the watchdog state is rendered back to a user, neither of which was read. No evidence of HTML output of this value was found in the readable span.

SiteGraph.render() builds a shell command string using Runtime.getRuntime().exec(String) with the user/config-supplied `output` directory concatenated directly into the command line:

    Runtime.getRuntime().exec("dot -o" + output + "/out.gif -Tgif " + output + "/out.dot");

The `output` value is never validated or quoted. On Java runtimes that implement Runtime.exec(String) by tokenizing with StringTokenizer (i.e. non-Windows, where it delegates to exec(String[]) via ProcessImpl/untokenized tokenization), shell metacharacters such as `;`, `&&`, `|` are not special, but whitespace splitting still allows additional arguments to be injected into the `dot` invocation and additional flag tokens (e.g. `-T...` or arbitrary dot flags) to be appended. The same `output` string is also used to construct a FileWriter path in prepare() (`output + "/out.dot"`) without path validation. The value originates from the command line argument parsed by getArg(args, "output") in main(), so it is developer/operator-controlled rather than attacker-controlled over the network in the normal usage documented in the class Javadoc (a build-time tool).

Root cause: JakartaMultiPartRequest builds its upload-error message by concatenating an exception-class-derived key and passing the exception's own message as the fallback string to LocalizedTextUtil.findText:

  protected String buildErrorMessage(Throwable e, Object[] args) {
      String errorKey = "struts.messages.upload.error." + e.getClass().getSimpleName();
      ...
      return LocalizedTextUtil.findText(this.getClass(), errorKey, defaultLocale, e.getMessage(), args);
  }

This method is invoked from parse(), whose catch block handles any Throwable/FileUploadException raised while parsing attacker supplied multipart input (parse():89-110 -> catch (Exception e) at 101 -> buildErrorMessage at 105).

Sink: LocalizedTextUtil.findText(Class,String,Locale,String,Object[]) (line 360) obtains the current ActionContext ValueStack and delegates to the 6-arg overload (line 408). When the bundle key is not found (the generated key like "struts.messages.upload.error.InvalidContentTypeException" is not present in a resource bundle for this class), control reaches getDefaultMessage(...) (lines 532-541 -> 648-671). There, if findDefaultText(key, locale) returns null, message is set to the supplied defaultMessage (line 657) and is then evaluated:

  MessageFormat mf = buildMessageFormat(TextParseUtil.translateVariables(message, valueStack), locale);  // line 663

TextParseUtil.translateVariables(...) (lines 47-49 -> 125-127 -> 153-167) constructs an OGNL evaluator whose evaluate() calls stack.findValue(parsedValue, asType) (line 157) for each %{...} / ${...} segment found by the TextParser.

Security impact: Commons FileUpload exceptions raised for malformed multipart requests include the request's Content-Type value verbatim in their getMessage() (e.g. InvalidContentTypeException). A crafted Content-Type header containing %{...} expression syntax therefore flows into the OGNL interpreter, enabling expression evaluation with the action's ValueStack (the documented multipart-parser RCE pattern). Because the flow originates in the dispatcher's multipart wrapping rather than in request parameters, it bypasses the parameters interceptor's OGNL/param-name restrictions.

Related observable-but-secondary sink: the same evaluate() result is also used to construct a MessageFormat whose pattern is the attacker influenced text (buildMessageFormat, lines 700-711), so malformed pattern text can also trigger a runtime exception; and messageFormats is a shared static cache keyed on (pattern, locale) (lines 856-887), so attacker unique patterns accumulate there (minor memory growth), but this is not the primary concern.
